+## 4. Toolchain
+
+- `rust-toolchain.toml` names a **floating nightly** for development and CI. Nightly
+  clippy and rustdoc carry lints that stable lacks, and floating means later
+  sharpening is never frozen into invisible debt. A new nightly can therefore raise
+  new lint findings in untouched code; they are fixed, not silenced. Do not select,
+  install or pin another compiler.
 - The **minimum supported Rust version is 1.98.1** (`rust-version` in the root
-  `Cargo.toml`), enforced on the stable channel. `make msrv` runs
-  `cargo check --workspace --all-targets --locked` through `rust-validate 1.98.1`,
-  and the same check on the fuzz harness manifest.
-- The source uses **no unstable features**. A `#![feature(...)]` attribute is
-  rejected; the MSRV build proves it on every change.
-- Raising the MSRV is a changelog-recorded change that rides a minor bump.
-- **Path containment is per platform.** `raf-source` verifies every handle it uses
-  against the workspace root, before the use and again after it, and reads a file's
-  contents only through a verified handle. On Linux a candidate is first taken as an
-  `O_PATH` reference and the kernel's path for it (`/proc`) must lie under the root
-  before it is opened for reading or listing through that path, so an outside
-  candidate is never opened for reading or listing. Linux retains the original
-  root directory description, validates its acquisition, and requires file
-  parent/name identity and retained-root ancestry before and after use. A root
-  replacement cannot retarget that tree; relocation outside its canonical scope
-  refuses. A clone of this directory handle is available to explicit metadata
-  publishers. These are observations, not an immutable snapshot.
-  On other Unix systems the path
-  is walked beneath a descriptor of the root with `openat` (through `rustix`), one
-  component at a time and never following a symlink, so no path is resolved by name
-  and an outside candidate is never opened; a walk outside the root may only
-  descend back into it, never climb, so a link cannot leave the root and return. On
-  Windows the path the system gives for the handle (`GetFinalPathNameByHandleW`,
-  through `windows-sys`, in the library's one `unsafe` block) must lie under the
-  root; an outside file can be opened in a race and is then refused before any
-  read, and a listing or an entry lookup goes by the handle's path, so a swap made
-  and undone between the two checks can return outside names or kinds, never
-  contents. Windows also refuses reserved device names, names ending in a dot or a
-  space, and names holding a colon. Standard validation runs only on Linux and
-  exercises only its native strategy. Cross-platform testing and cross-compilation
-  are forbidden. Other platform adapters remain in source without standard
-  validation claims.
-- **Primary target through the 0.9 release.** Implementation and qualification
-  cover the primary native Linux target. Other operating systems,
-  secondary architectures and cross-compilation are subject to reassessment
-  after that release; they are not current completion requirements. Existing
-  adapters may remain without support claims. Native Cargo target kinds and
-  compiler versions remain covered. See [`docs/TARGETS.md`](docs/TARGETS.md).
+  `Cargo.toml`), checked on the stable channel by `make msrv` for the workspace and
+  the fuzz harness. Raising it is a changelog-recorded change on a minor bump.
+- The source uses **no unstable features**; a `#![feature(...)]` attribute is
+  rejected.
+- `syn`, `proc-macro2` and `quote` use exact shared requirements in
+  `[workspace.dependencies]`, mirrored in the fuzz workspace, because the native
+  parser resource bounds were measured on those implementations. A consumer must
+  align its requirements with these pins; conflicting requirements fail during Cargo
+  resolution. Changing a pin requires inspecting the replacement and re-measuring.
+- PurRDF crates come from crates.io with the requirement `=4`.
+- **Path containment** in `raf-source` verifies every handle against the workspace
+  root before and after use, and reads contents only through a verified handle. Only
+  the Linux strategy is validated. The other platform adapters stay in the source
+  without validation claims, and cross-platform testing and cross-compilation are
+  not done. The source-index capability states the per-platform contract.
 
